WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.720
Human neurogenesis lasts months, not the mouse's week.

2
00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:09.040
Where might extra divisions happen?

3
00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:12.080
And why wouldn't they simply make our cortex thick?

4
00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:16.590
Rakic built his model around progenitors at the ventricle.

5
00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:20.710
But in fetal monkey cortex, the zone above them is split in two.

6
00:00:21.733 --> 00:00:23.613
What was dividing in the outer part?

7
00:00:24.583 --> 00:00:30.283
In 2010, Hansen, Kriegstein and colleagues filmed living slices of human fetal

8
00:00:30.283 --> 00:00:31.283
cortex.

9
00:00:31.866 --> 00:00:36.846
They found cells with the markers of radial glia, the proteins PAX6 and SOX2.

10
00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:42.620
These cells kept a fibre toward the brain surface, but had no process touching the

11
00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:43.620
ventricle.

12
00:00:43.983 --> 00:00:47.183
Before dividing, a cell moved its cell body up its fibre.

13
00:00:48.223 --> 00:00:52.383
Daughters could divide again, or become intermediate progenitors, which soon make

14
00:00:52.383 --> 00:00:53.383
neurons.

15
00:00:53.866 --> 00:00:55.746
These are outer radial glia.

16
00:00:56.626 --> 00:01:00.766
Later imaging in fetal macaque found them making proliferative divisions too.

17
00:01:01.766 --> 00:01:05.086
So, in macaque at least, they can be a second site of doubling.

18
00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:08.010
How much can a little doubling matter?

19
00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:09.930
Take a toy lineage.

20
00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:14.630
One progenitor divides for 60 rounds, and each division adds a neuron.

21
00:01:15.733 --> 00:01:17.093
That makes 60 neurons.

22
00:01:18.116 --> 00:01:24.356
Predict. Now, in every round, give each progenitor a one in ten chance of making two

23
00:01:24.356 --> 00:01:25.356
progenitors instead.

24
00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.260
Every progenitor keeps dividing.

25
00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:33.000
On average, do you get about 60 neurons, a few hundred, or thousands?

26
00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:40.930
Thousands, on average.

27
00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:46.490
Each doubling adds a progenitor that keeps dividing, so the pool grows by a tenth

28
00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:48.590
each round, like compound interest.

29
00:01:49.633 --> 00:01:53.013
Given enough rounds, a little doubling outgrows a lot of adding.

30
00:01:57.416 --> 00:01:59.036
So why no thicker cortex?

31
00:02:01.536 --> 00:02:03.596
New neurons climb radial glial fibres.

32
00:02:04.536 --> 00:02:08.936
If every fibre ran straight up, with packing fixed, what would extra neurons do?

33
00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:19.860
In this cartoon, they would stack onto existing columns, and the cortex would

34
00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:20.860
thicken.

35
00:02:21.183 --> 00:02:25.403
Instead, the fibres diverge as they climb, fanning the scaffold outward.

36
00:02:26.323 --> 00:02:29.003
That favours spreading the neurons sideways, into area.

37
00:02:33.466 --> 00:02:36.266
Outer radial glia are not uniquely human.

38
00:02:37.286 --> 00:02:39.706
Mice have a few, and ferrets and primates have many.

39
00:02:40.716 --> 00:02:44.276
The human outer subventricular zone is especially large.

40
00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:48.740
The marmoset, whose cortex is nearly smooth, has them too.

41
00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:51.420
So they are not enough for folding on their own.

42
00:02:52.383 --> 00:02:54.583
Founders can double at the ventricle.

43
00:02:55.483 --> 00:03:00.103
Outer radial glia can double far from it, and their fanned fibres favour area over

44
00:03:00.103 --> 00:03:00.463
thickness.
